# Tailwind CSS Conventions

## Verify CSS Classes Exist Before Using

Before adding or changing Tailwind utility classes, verify the class actually exists in the compiled CSS (`src/index.css`). This project uses a pre-compiled Tailwind CSS output — not all utility classes are available.

```tsx
// BAD: Using shorthand/fractional classes that don't exist in compiled CSS
<div className="my-8 mb-10 gap-2.5">

// GOOD: Using individual classes that are verified to exist
<div className="mt-8 mb-8 gap-3">
```

Known missing classes (NOT in compiled CSS): `my-*`, `mb-10`, `gap-2.5`, `gap-5`.
Available gap classes: `gap-1`, `gap-1.5`, `gap-2`, `gap-3`, `gap-4`, `gap-6`, `gap-8`, `gap-12`.
Available margin-bottom: `mb-1` thru `mb-8`, `mb-12`, `mb-16` (no `mb-10`).

When unsure, search `src/index.css` for the class (e.g., grep for `.my-8` or `.gap-2.5`) before using it.

## Never Stack Duplicate or Conflicting Classes

Do not add new classes on top of existing ones that target the same CSS property. Always replace — never accumulate.

```tsx
// BAD: Stacking conflicting margin classes
<div className="mt-4 mt-8">

// GOOD: Single, correct value
<div className="mt-8">
```

## Preserve Existing Code Structure

When making styling changes:
- Replace the specific class that needs changing; do not rewrite the entire className string
- Do not remove unrelated classes
- Do not add classes without verifying they won't conflict with existing ones
